    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d137">

                <pb facs="0201" n="187" xml:id="pg187"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.248/10–1248: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Holmes</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-10-12T18:00:00+01:00">October 12, 1948—6
                            p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4472. Tehran’s 1174 October 5 to Department<anchor n="1" xml:id="d137-f1"/>.
                    Reference jets for Iran. Pyman said October 11 that he recently attended meeting
                    [on] Iran [of] Chiefs of Staff who decided that through joint staff mission
                    Washington it would shortly communicate to US Chiefs of Staff that UK unable to
                    produce large number jets for Iran in near future. However, in order not to
                    dissipate entirely political advantage of Shah’s visit to UK they would like to
                    make available a few jets not to exceed six which Iran might hope to purchase
                    from manufacturer and receive delivery by end 1950 which, in any case is as
                    early as Iranians could be trained to fly jets. According Pyman, British Chiefs
                    of Staff will probably suggest to US counterpart further US–UK consultation on
                    jets and inquire what US has found it possible to do for Iran regarding jets. In
                    course such consultation British Chiefs may suggest that each country should
                    tell Shah what it can do in this connection and let Shah choose (paragraph <hi
                        rendition="#italic">c</hi> Tehran’s reftel).</p>
                <p>[Here follows paragraph numbered two which advised primarily that the British had
                    no intention of sending an air training mission to Iran.]</p>
                <p>Sent Department 4472, repeated Tehran 85.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Holmes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
